    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L18-ACTIVITY-004",
      "SourceSection": "18.2: Be Discriminating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Kiran was using the quadratic formula to solve the equation \\(x^2 - 12x + 41 = 0\\). He wrote this:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L18-ACTIVITY-005",
      "SourceSection": "18.2: Be Discriminating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x = \\dfrac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 164}}{2}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L18-ACTIVITY-006",
      "SourceSection": "18.2: Be Discriminating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Then he noticed that the number inside the square root is negative and said, “This equation doesn’t have any solutions.”",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L18-ACTIVITY-007",
      "SourceSection": "18.2: Be Discriminating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Do you agree with Kiran? Explain your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L18-ACTIVITY-008",
      "SourceSection": "18.2: Be Discriminating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Write \\(\\sqrt{\\text- 20}\\) as an imaginary number.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L18-ACTIVITY-009",
      "SourceSection": "18.2: Be Discriminating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Solve the equation \\(3x^2 - 10x +50=0\\) and plot the solutions in the complex plane.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L18-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "18.2: Be Discriminating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Although imaginary numbers let us describe complex solutions to quadratic equations, they were actually discovered and accepted because they could help us find real solutions to equations with polynomials of degree 3. In the 16th century,…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L18-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "18.2: Be Discriminating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "To find a solution to the equation \\(x^3-px-q=0\\) the cubic formula would first tell us to find a complex number, \\(z\\), which is \\(\\frac{q}{2}+i\\sqrt{(\\frac{p}{3})^3-(\\frac{q}{2})^2}\\). Find \\(z\\) when our equation is \\(x^3-15x-4=0\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L18-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "18.2: Be Discriminating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The next step is to find a complex number \\(w\\) so that \\(w^3=z\\). Show that \\(w=2+i\\) works for the \\(z\\) we found in step 1.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L18-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "18.2: Be Discriminating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "If we write \\(w=a+bi\\) where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are real numbers, the solutions to our equation are \\(2a\\), \\(\\text-a+b\\sqrt{3}\\), and \\(\\text-a-b\\sqrt{3}\\). What are the three solutions to our equation \\(x^3-15x-4=0\\)?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L18-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Solving All Kinds of Quadratics",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For each row, you and your partner will each solve a quadratic equation. You should each get the same answer. If you disagree, work to reach agreement.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L18-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Solving All Kinds of Quadratics",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Sometimes when we use the quadratic formula to solve a quadratic equation, we get a negative number inside the square root symbol. This means that the solutions to the equation must involve imaginary numbers. For example, consider the…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L18-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Solving All Kinds of Quadratics",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle 5x^2 + x + 10 = 0\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
